  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 306 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe upper level trough currently over the West Coast moves east \nacross the Rockies and into the Great Plains by Wednesday night. The \nwave is pushed northeast by the strong upper ridge over the East \nCoast and moves across the Upper Midwest into the northern Great \nLakes region Wednesday night and Thursday.  The surface reflection \nof the wave pushes a cold front through the Upper Midwest into Iowa \nwhere it runs headlong into the surface high and stalls Thursday \nafternoon.  Deterministic guidance generated a few tenths of an inch \nof precip ahead of this front in our area a few days ago, but \nsubsequent model runs have come in drier and drier.  The latest GFS \nand ECMWF show no precip for our area with this system Wednesday \nnight into Thursday with the ridge axis to our south blocking any \nsignificant moisture return.  Temperatures continue to rise a few \ndegrees Thursday though Saturday though, with afternoon highs \ntopping out in the upper 60s to around 70 by Saturday afternoon. \nGuidance shows the next upstream short wave moving into the Great \nPlains Saturday/Saturday night.  This wave continues to look \nstronger than the previous one.  It finally weakens the ridge over \nthe eastern U.S. which allows low level Gulf moisture to flow up \ninto the Mid Mississippi Valley on Saturday.  However, the latest \nglobal guidance is pretty miserly with the moisture return, and the \nlow level moisture convergence ahead of the surface cold front is \nweaker than previously forecast.  QPF in the ECMWF And GFS has \nfallen to less than 0.10 inch for most of the area.  The latest LREF \nstill shows a 50-60 percent chance of measurable precip along and \nahead of the front, mainly on Saturday night.  NBM PoPs top out at \n40-50 percent, which may be a little low based on LREF \nprobabilities, however the trend is for a drier forecast, so no \nchanges are anticipated at this point.  Another cool high pressure \nsystem builds into the Mississippi Valley behind the cold front \nSunday into Monday.  LREF cluster analysis continues to show \nvariance in the amplitude and position of the trough driving the \ncold front through the area, as well as the upstream ridge as it \nbuilds into the Central U.S. This leads to uncertainty in how much \ntemperatures will fall behind the front, and high temperature IQRs \non Sunday and Monday are 8-10 degrees.  However, it should be \nnoticeably cooler as the entire range of forecast maxes from the \n10th to 90th percentile is lower behind the front.",
